{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness","id":1093091,"title":"H.P Lovecraft and the World of Darkness","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-15T20:59:02-07:00","id":1093091,"title":"H.P Lovecraft and the World of Darkness","content":"I been fascinated with this author's work for a long time. I know theres constant references in the Inspiration suggestions of different books. but i was wondering about more Direct connections, such as Cthulhu, Kadath, The Mountains of Madness and so on..So..Which direct homages can we find in the different books?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Forum's Official's Joker and Trickster. Pardon my bad english, aint my first language (I Speak Spanish).<br /> ST: DtF, HtR, WtO, MtA<br /> Signature Chars: Crowley (hakalu), Joe The Nuwisha (WtA)<br /> Changelings: be afraid of the Technocracian High Five of Doom","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1754-crowley","name":"Crowley","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1754_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1639"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T21:46:42-07:00","id":1093100,"title":"","content":"In Mage an entire faction of the Nephandi is made up of Lovecraft-style crazy cultists who worship incomprehensible abominations from beyond the stars.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T22:32:10-07:00","id":1093109,"title":"","content":"There's a Cthulhu-like being in <i>the Book of Madness</i> 2nd edition (looming over a Labyrinth) and in <i>M20</i><i> Core</i> (being summoned).<br /> <br /> Also, check out Robert W. Chamber's <i>The King in Yellow</i> - it's very good and connected to Lovecraft's Mythos.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-15T23:06:27-07:00","id":1093121,"title":"","content":"Book of the Wyrm is in some chapters inspired by / maby connected with Lovercraftian myths.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1784-werewolf43","name":"werewolf43","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1784_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"315"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T01:08:31-07:00","id":1093143,"title":"","content":"Well, I write Lovecraftian fiction for a living so I think the best gameline for Lovecraftian fiction is Mage (Nephandi) and Werewolf (Wyrm creatures).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T01:36:11-07:00","id":1093150,"title":"","content":"There are some fairly Lovecraft-esk things in blood dimmed tides.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T02:05:39-07:00","id":1093156,"title":"","content":"A example character of the Clanbook: Tzimisce 2nd edition is a scholar of the Mythos.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2250-magus","name":"Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T08:05:07-07:00","id":1093261,"title":"","content":"In Rokea, Qyrl finds something she calls &quot;Nightmare&quot; that she wishes into the exact same presentation of form for which Cthulhu is known. She is then forced by Sea to shrink it down to size, resulting in a being which gave rise to the Thallain merfolk known as merrow or merdhuacha, depending on which edition you're using.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T19:14:52-07:00","id":1093494,"title":"","content":"There are no direct references to Lovecraftiana in the games, but there are various indirect references where the author was clearly inspired by Lovecraft or other mythos authors. As others have mentioned, the most frequent references are in Mage with the Nephandi and Werewolf in regards to the Wyrm with some others in Vampire. Presumably, Changeling might have some references to the Dreamlands stories, but I've read very little Changeling. Here are some things that may be reference to Lovecraft,<br /> <br /> 1) There seems to be something in Antarctica which some Garou are interested in. Possibly a reference to the Mountains of Madness.<br /> 2) Various Necronomicon like books, usually in Mage but Werewolf has them too. Especially the Malleus Nefandorum and the Liber Labyrinthus.<br /> 3) The Children of the Baali could be based on certain interpretations.<br /> 4) Many of the Black Spiral Dancer Totems seem inspired by the Mythos - Relshab and G'lough seem like common Mythos concepts.<br /> 5) Vhujunka seem to be inspired by the Serpent People.<br /> 6) There is a direct reference to the Carpenter film The Prince of Darkness (Exhibit 338: the Anti-Christ Serum in Book of the Weaver) which is Lovecraft one removed.<br /> 7) Blood Dim Ties has references to a Cthulhu like creature, and the Chluroviah are clearly based on Deep Ones (and Starro the Conqueror of DC comics!)<br /> <br /> Those are just the ones on the tip of my head.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3046-black-fox","name":"Black Fox","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4187"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-16T20:44:45-07:00","id":1093520,"title":"","content":"Of course, the problem with direct parallels is that cWoD, even WtA, possesses too much optimism. Humans are nothing more than a passing curiosity to the creatures of Lovecraft. They are delusional and are incapable of accurately perceiving Reality without going insane because of a failure in their evolution. While the creatures of Lovecraft may entertain themselves through abusing humans, the majority of them have existed for millions or billions of years before humanity and will exist millions or billions of years after humanity. There is also no grand end in the cosmos of Lovecraft (the Universe existed eternally before humanity and will continue to exist forever after humanity is gone).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-20T09:03:33-07:00","id":1094878,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness?p=1093520#post1093520\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Of course, the problem with direct parallels is that cWoD, even WtA, possesses too much optimism. Humans are nothing more than a passing curiosity to the creatures of Lovecraft. They are delusional and are incapable of accurately perceiving Reality without going insane because of a failure in their evolution. While the creatures of Lovecraft may entertain themselves through abusing humans, the majority of them have existed for millions or billions of years before humanity and will exist millions or billions of years after humanity. There is also no grand end in the cosmos of Lovecraft (the Universe existed eternally before humanity and will continue to exist forever after humanity is gone).</div> </div> </div> </div>I see this less as a bug, and more like a feature. The World of Darkness, for all its eponymous darkness, is a setting where the Lovecraftian concept of a tiny world in a larger, uncaring universe is directly opposed by and competed with that world's inhabitants, and their own conceptions. The dissonance between themes and outlooks is intentional. With Mage especially, but in all the games, it's a war of world views. And it plays out everywhere, this conflict between small beings and seemingly unstoppable abominations beyond them.<br /> <br /> In Vampire, young Kindred fight against the machinations of their elders, including the godlike antedeluvians; trying to impose their own self will upon a world filled with puppet masters, schemers, and domitors. In Werewolf, it's a conflict between the Shapechangers, who represent our little world, and the cosmic forces that have gone mad (or, in the case of the Wyld, were arguably always mad). In Mage, the Awakened fight against each other to define what Reality is, but come together to combat the machinations of their heartless kin, and the blasphemous things from outside the Tellurian that seek to plunder it. In Changeling, those touched by old fae souls - many of them mere children - must contend with an ever-thickening blanket of Banality, that would smother the world and kill the dreams of men. In Wraith, the opponent of every ghost is nothing short of Oblivion itself, followed shortly after by their own personified existential despair, and the lingering doubts about whether there is a true afterlife to look forward to<br /> <br /> To walk the difficult path - to push against these seemingly irresistible, transcendent forces - is to affirm one's own importance in Reality, no matter how small. Lovecraft posits an Objective view of the universe as a huge place, filled with monsters and gods and gods that are also monsters. Too huge for any small mind to grasp. The World of Darkness sees that argument, gives it a fair consideration, then throws it back in Lovecraft's face, displaying its own Subjective Reality. In a world where - impossibly - two contradictory truths could both be correct, at the same time and in the same sense, the small things that dwell on this tiny planet can reject Lovecraft's reality, and substitute their own.<br /> <br /> A young lick CAN devour a Methusalah. Werewolves CAN stymie the Wyrm. Mages DO tell the Great Ones to get off their property, and HAVE killed a blood god. The point where the competing Paradigms - Lovecraft's and the people of Earth - collide, THAT is where drama comes in.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-20T10:11:37-07:00","id":1094885,"title":"","content":"Yes, which is why cWoD cannot support Lovecraftian games. Permanent victories are impossible in a Lovecraftian reality, while they are very much possible in cWoD. A Lovecraftian cWoD would be a world full of delusional creatures who think that their sacrifices are meaningful in the long run. That being said, WA is probably the closest because the Garou know that they are doomed but fight anyway because they would rather die fighting than die running away.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-20T14:32:00-07:00","id":1094944,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness?p=1094885#post1094885\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> That being said, WA is probably the closest because the Garou know that they are doomed but fight anyway because they would rather die fighting than die running away.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Except that WtA, along with all of the CWoD gamelines, run of the assumption that the universe is malefic, not indifferent. <br /> <br /> The Wyrm wants to destroy the earth, the Vampires need humanity to survive, and Mages NEED something to believe in for their magic to work (unless they go Nephandi, but even then they suffer the same Malefic fallacy the Wyrm does.) The World of Darkness is so earth and human centric that every gamelines emphasizes how important humanity is and how precious and special our world is. This isn't Lovecraftian. In a Lovecraftian game setting the earth and every single human and supernatural creature on it regardless of gameline would be ultimately inconsequential. The battles they fight would be ultimately meaningless because they are so insignificant on a cosmic scale that they might as well have never happened at all. <br /> <br /> And for that to work, the universe itself must be indifferent, not Malefic. The word corruption is meaningless (and pointless) in the Mythos, because corruption only exists in a world where evil exists. And the Classic World of Darkness would not be the Classic World of Darkness without that Malefic undercurrent to every aspect of the gamelines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-20T14:48:49-07:00","id":1094951,"title":"","content":"Indifference can be malefic. Lovecraft used the word corruption (or its synonyms) in every one of his stories, so he obviously thought that the universe was malefic in its indifference. Humans were insects to the forces of the Mythos, who would pull the arms off a child with the same indifference of a child pulling the wings of a fly.<br /> <br /> The reason why WtA is closer to Lovecraft than the other gamelines is that the actions of the Garou are meaningless. We only have the perspective of the Garou concerning the significance of the Supernals, and the Supernals could be local phenomena as meaningless to the Universe as individual humans are to them. Of course, an ST can say that the Apocalypse is universally significant, but I prefer to think that it is just a local matter and that everything in WtA is insignificant to the rest of the Universe.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-20T17:40:17-07:00","id":1094975,"title":"","content":"Two things first.<br /> <br /> 1) The latter point about personal significance &amp; actual significance is spot on in regards to WtA. Well said.<br /> <br /> 2) I made a mistake, malefic isn't the right word I'm looking for.<br /> <br /> My point is that the phenomena &amp; creatures of Lovecraft's work don't adhere to conventional ideas of good &amp; evil. Classic World of Darkness very much does even in WtA due to very clear Judeo Christian undertones (Werewolf the &quot;Apocalypse&quot;) This idea that there IS cosmic Evil automatically makes a true Lovecraftian story impossible because the Great Old Ones never acted purposefully malevolently. The closest one to ever do so is Nyrlathotep, and even then everything human about the character is a intentional facade to represent humanity's insistence upon trying to understand the incomprehensible. <br /> <br /> It is a matter of point though that in Lovecraft's stories the main source of malevolence comes from human characters, or &quot;sentient human-like entities&quot; like the Elder Things from the Mountains of Madness. Malevolence is more accurately ambitious self-interest at the cost of all else in the Mythos, where people foolishly try to wield powers they couldn't hope to control on order to stave off death or reshape the world to their own desires. And while CWoD does do a pretty fantastic job of this (and is truly the closest it comes to being Lovecraftian) it kneecaps itself by making the &quot;dark forces&quot; truly malevolent entities rather than cosmically indifferent forces that barely register the existence of earth rather than greedily hungering for it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-20T20:19:51-07:00","id":1095001,"title":"","content":"Well, it depends on your interpretations of the stories. Yes, evil and corruption are human things (or humanoid things) but that is because the Elder Gods and Great Old Ones are so powerful that they step on humans much like humans might step on ants when crossing a field. Do they intend to step on humans? No. Do they care that they step on humans? No. Would they avoid stepping on humans if they noticed them? No. They are evil in their indifference to humans because they are willing to destroy humans (even all of humanity) without a second thought. Earth does seem to have some minor importance in the Lovercraftian stories though, even if humans do not, as it seems to be a cosmic meeting place where lots of things end up.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-20T21:28:00-07:00","id":1095016,"title":"","content":"I think we might be talking about two different Mythoses here. Because in the works of Lovecraft I've read the Great Old Ones don't actively try to destroy humanity. They do so without even realizing humanity is there, like how just by entering a room your ambient body temperature changes the temperature of the room...except on a cosmic scale. They're more like forces of nature, and as terrifying and destructive as they are calling them evil is like calling a tornado or earthquake evil.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3436-dusksage","name":"Dusksage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3436_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"869"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-21T04:46:41-07:00","id":1095083,"title":"","content":"Wraith has Tindel Hounds, but its more a name easter egg.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/754-eldagusto","name":"Eldagusto","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar754_2.gif","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6435"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-21T05:29:00-07:00","id":1095091,"title":"","content":"No, we are reading the same stories, I just think we have different definitions of evil. Unlike a hurricane or tornado, the Great Old Ones are sapient and have the ability to choose to not do harm to other sapient (the Great Old Ones have enough experience with humanity to know that humans are sapient). The fact that they choose to not minimize the harm that they do to humans makes the Great Old Ones evil and malefic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11683-aya-tari","name":"Aya Tari","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5986"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-21T08:55:22-07:00","id":1095147,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness?p=1095091#post1095091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, we are reading the same stories, I just think we have different definitions of evil. Unlike a hurricane or tornado, the Great Old Ones are sapient and have the ability to choose to not do harm to other sapient (the Great Old Ones have enough experience with humanity to know that humans are sapient). The fact that they choose to not minimize the harm that they do to humans makes the Great Old Ones evil and malefic.</div> </div> </div> </div>Agreed. And I don't think that your definition of evil is definitely on target. IIndifference when you cause harm is, in fact, a hallmark of of certain values of &quot;evil&quot;. An example on the mundane and human level:<br /> <br /> A serial killer comes into a family's home and brutally murders all of them.<br /> <br /> A landlord evicts a family during the most brutal part of winter. They have nowhere to go and they all freeze to death. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1062-ajax","name":"Ajax","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1062_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-21T09:35:48-07:00","id":1095160,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aya Tari</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness?p=1095091#post1095091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, we are reading the same stories, I just think we have different definitions of evil. Unlike a hurricane or tornado, the Great Old Ones are sapient and have the ability to choose to not do harm to other sapient (the Great Old Ones have enough experience with humanity to know that humans are sapient). The fact that they choose to not minimize the harm that they do to humans makes the Great Old Ones evil and malefic.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's like people walking down the street.<br /> <br /> Humans know there are ants on the ground. And we know that when we step on ants, they die. But most people walk down the street looking up and ahead, and don't bother looking down. If they step on a bunch of ants, well, tough luck. There are humans who look down as they walk, and try to avoid stepping on ants, but those people tend to be a bit unusual.<br /> <br /> There's no doubt that from the ant's perspective, the humans are evil and malefic creatures who simply step on them. Even the humans who try to avoid them are still scary and dangerous because they make the earth shake and sometimes they step on ants anyway. And ultimately to the humans, even the ones who try to avoid stepping on ants, ants just ants, and is anyone really going to lose any sleep over stepping on a few?<br /> <br /> That's how I tend to imagine the Great Old Ones - from the human perspective they're absolutely evil, but from their own perspective humans are completely insignificant.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/741-anubisxy","name":"AnubisXy","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar741_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5693"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-21T13:42:38-07:00","id":1095224,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Crowley</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness#post1093091\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I been fascinated with this author's work for a long time. I know theres constant references in the Inspiration suggestions of different books. but i was wondering about more Direct connections, such as Cthulhu, Kadath, The Mountains of Madness and so on..So..Which direct homages can we find in the different books?</div> </div> </div> </div>The main ones from back in the day were both editions of Book of the Wyrm, both editions of Book of Madness, Mage's Book of Chantries, most mentions of the Oburtus revenant family, Mage's Book of Mirrors, and the stuff about the Neverborn in Wraith.<br /> <br /> Also: If Pulp Era Mage had ever been a thing, the Marauder signature character would've been based on Randolph Carter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 05-21-2017, 04:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-21T22:31:24-07:00","id":1095367,"title":"","content":"Demon: The Earthbound is chock-full of tongue-in-cheek Lovecraftian references and actively encourages those sorts of themes and imagery in implementing/designing Earthbound. The Neverborn Malfeans out of Mage/Wraith and the Children of the Baali are likewise right up Lovecraft's alley; Clanbook Baali, the Book of Madness, Spectres: Dark Reflections, and the Sea of Shadows can all provide some inspiration for those.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10809-andkat","name":"Andkat","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"181"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-22T15:30:20-07:00","id":1095635,"title":"","content":"There's also a little bit in <i>Infernalism: Path of Screams</i>, though most of that book is concerned with the medieval conception of evil: demons, Hell, and the witches devoted to them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-22T16:19:35-07:00","id":1095647,"title":"","content":"I'd also forgotten The Chronicle of the Black Labyrinth, about half of which are straight up homages to fiction by Lovecraft, Blackwood and others.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-29T01:25:06-07:00","id":1098019,"title":"","content":"I would say all the books most likely have a smattering of lovecraft references and themes. Unknowable things and secrets which just knowing that they exist border on madness","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/359-dezeroth","name":"Dezeroth","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar359_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"496"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-30T07:38:14-07:00","id":1098426,"title":"","content":"The book of the Yellow Sign is mentioned as a dark grimoire in the supplement of the Red Sign.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2250-magus","name":"Magus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2250_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-30T08:12:57-07:00","id":1098436,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=FI6b5GP8TPY\" data-code=\"FI6b5GP8TPY\" data-provider=\"youtube\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/FI6b5GP8TPY/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div><br /> <br /> I asked Lovecraft himself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Onyx Path Kickstarters I back.<br /> W20, MtC, CB20, DtD, MtAs20, BotW20, Wraith 20th.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1442-paladindemo","name":"PaladinDemo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1442_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"622"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-31T13:11:23-07:00","id":1098856,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Magus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness?p=1098426#post1098426\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The book of the Yellow Sign is mentioned as a dark grimoire in the supplement of the Red Sign.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's really a homage to Chambers, although the Necronomicon Ex Mortis is another of the books dealt with in that product. (OK, so it's the <i>Evil Dead</i> take on Lovecraft's Necronomicon. Less summoning incomprehensible things from beyond the stars, more getting oneself into Monkey's Paw type situations with reanimated loved ones...)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3505-matt-the-bruins-fan","name":"Matt the Bruins fan","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3505_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2401"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-17T05:37:24-07:00","id":1141028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Matt the Bruins fan</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness?p=1098856#post1098856\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That's really a homage to Chambers</div> </div> </div> </div>It could be a homage to both, as the Yellow Sign, and Hastur, are mentioned in <i>The Whisperer in Darkness</i> by H.P Lovecraft.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3254-muad-dib","name":"Muad'Dib","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3254_3.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2059"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-18T08:24:59-07:00","id":1141412,"title":"","content":"just adding something<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness?p=1093494#post1093494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) There seems to be something in Antarctica which some Garou are interested in. Possibly a reference to the Mountains of Madness.</div> </div> </div> </div>-the Nosferatu Antediluvian was rumored to rest in Antarctica<br /> -apparently, there was a giant hive of Black Spirals geographycally located under Antarctica but accessible mainly by spirit tracks<br /> -in the Time of Judgement, the last message from a research station in Antarctica il &quot;the gods of blood are here&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Black Fox</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/1093091-h-p-lovecraft-and-the-world-of-darkness?p=1093494#post1093494\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3) The Children of the Baali could be based on certain interpretations.</div> </div> </div> </div>-the old capital of the Baali, Chorazin, is mentioned several times in the Lovercraft novels<br /> -while all the infernalist in the WoD seems like the cultists in Lovecraft, the Baali seems exceptionally adept at playing the role.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>====================================<br /> &quot;Worst English-language User&quot; 2011-2015, 2017 running for the 2018<br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\"><br /> <br /> <img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://i.imgur.com/Ed6RV6Z.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></div></div> </div> </div>","edited":{"userid":515,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/515-ryoga84","user":"Ryoga84","date":". 09-18-2017, 11:28 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/515-ryoga84","name":"Ryoga84","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/